# Additional permissions

Video-Code is licensed under the PolyForm Noncommercial License 1.0.0, printed
below word for word. That license alone would make a monetised video a
commercial use, which would empty the tool of its purpose. The three permissions
here are granted by Marius Rousset on top of it. They add to the license; they
take nothing away from it, and they change nothing in its text.

## 1. The videos you make with it

Rendering a video with Video-Code, and publishing it, is a permitted purpose --
including where the video earns money: advertising, sponsorship, memberships,
paid courses, or a film you sell. Every platform counts, and so does every
sponsor.

The condition is the credit. The video, or the page that carries it, must name
Video-Code and the address given under "License" in `README.md`. The address
lives in the README so that it can change without breaking videos already
published.

If you forget the credit, you have not lost anything: you have 30 days from a
written notice to put it back, and this permission continues.

**Not covered: a video made as paid work for someone else.** This permission
covers what you publish under your own name. A video produced to order is
business use, which permission 3 covers.

## 2. The scenes, templates and agent code you write

A scene is a Python file that imports this library; so is a template, and so is
the code an AI agent writes to drive it. They are yours. Publish them, share
them, sell them, license them as you like -- this project claims nothing in
them, and the noncommercial terms below do not reach them.

Whoever runs your scene still needs their own license to Video-Code itself.

## 3. Business use under 100 000 EUR of revenue

Using Video-Code in the course of business -- a video produced to order for a
client, the tool inside a service or a product you sell -- is a permitted
purpose for as long as your revenue stays under 100 000 EUR.

Your revenue is the gross revenue -- turnover, not profit -- of you and of
every organisation you control or that controls you, added together over the
last twelve months, every currency converted at the rate of the day. All of it
counts, not only the part Video-Code helped earn. You judge it yourself; no one
will ask for your books.

The credit of permission 1 applies to every video you publish under this
permission too.

Above the threshold you need a commercial license. Its price is a fixed yearly
sum per organisation, published under "License" in `README.md` -- there for the
same reason the credit address is: so it can change without this text
changing. Write to <rousset.marius13@gmail.com> to take one. Crossing the
threshold gives you 90 days to do so, and nothing you did before stays in
question.

## Duration

These three permissions are irrevocable, except for someone who breaks the terms
below and does not fix it within the 32 days that license allows.

